post_install() {
  # The initramfs must be rebuilt so the hook is present. On EVERY machine —
  # a machine whose initramfs lacked it would be distinguishable from every
  # other SteelOS machine, which is a finding even for a user who will never
  # configure duress.
  if command -v mkinitcpio >/dev/null; then
    grep -q 'steel-duress' /etc/mkinitcpio.conf 2>/dev/null || \
      sed -i 's/^HOOKS=(\(.*\)encrypt/HOOKS=(\1steel-duress encrypt/' /etc/mkinitcpio.conf 2>/dev/null || true
    mkinitcpio -P >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
  fi

  cat <<'MSG'

steel-duress: installed. Nothing is configured, and that is the normal state.

  This package is on EVERY SteelOS machine whether or not anyone uses it.
  That is deliberate: a duress package installed only by people who need
  duress would make its own presence the evidence. Finding it here proves
  only that this machine runs SteelOS.

  BEFORE configuring anything, read:

      docs/duress-and-deniability.md

  Specifically, that these measures hide WHETHER you used a decoy and not
  THAT the capability exists; that an adversary who images your disk twice
  defeats a decoy outright; and that destroying data during an investigation
  is itself an offence in some places.

  For most people at risk, not carrying the data at all beats every trick in
  that document.

MSG
}

post_upgrade() {
  command -v mkinitcpio >/dev/null && mkinitcpio -P >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
}

pre_remove() {
  cat <<'MSG'

steel-duress: REMOVING THIS PACKAGE IS A DENIABILITY PROBLEM.

  Every other SteelOS machine has it. A machine without it is distinguishable
  from a stock install to anyone who looks, and "why did you remove the duress
  package" is a worse conversation than "every install has one".

  If you do not want duress features, simply do not configure them. That is
  the intended state and it is indistinguishable from this package being
  present and unused — which is the entire point.

MSG
}
